.docname {Claims Schema}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Crate | A Rust library other Rust code builds against | `claims-schema` |
    | Claims header | `x-gateway-claims`: the checked token's claims, base64-encoded, added by the edge | `eyJzdWIiOiJ1MSJ9` |
    | Base64 alphabet | The 64 characters base64 uses, and whether it pads with `=` | standard uses `+ /`, URL-safe uses `- _` |
    | Extractor | Axum code that builds a value from a request before the handler runs | `GatewayClaims` |
    | Lenient | Keeps what it can read instead of failing the whole thing | a bad membership is dropped |
    | Fixture | A JSON file of example messages every language must agree on | `schemas/fixtures/standard-claims.json` |

Rust crate at `schemas/claims-schema`. It reads the `x-gateway-claims` header for every Rust service. Go has the same job in [Auth Contracts](auth-contracts.qd), Python in `agent/app/auth.py`: one reader per language, never one per service.

Depends only on `serde`, `serde_json`, `base64`. No I/O, no web framework.

---

## Who uses it

| Service | Imports | Rule its extractor adds |
|---|---|---|
| `digital-twin` | `ClaimsPayload`, `Membership`, `CLAIMS_HEADER`, `decode_claims_header` | `sub` required, else 401 `Invalid authentication token` |
| `telemetry` | same | `sub` required |
| `dashboard` | `ClaimsPayload::decode`, `CLAIMS_HEADER` | `sub` required, else bare `401` |
| `chat` | `ClaimsPayload`, `CLAIMS_HEADER`, `decode_claims_header` | `sub` required, else 401 `…missing an account id` |
| `notification` | same as `digital-twin` | `accountName` required, else 401 `…missing an account` |
| `socket` | `ClaimsPayload::decode`, `CLAIMS_HEADER` | `sub` **and** `accountName` required, else the connection is refused |

The crate only reads. Deciding which field is required is each service's job, because the services need different things: `notification` keys subscriptions by account name, the others key on `sub`.

---

## Shape

```rust
pub const CLAIMS_HEADER: &str = "x-gateway-claims";

pub struct Membership {
    pub domain: String,
    pub role: Option<String>,
    pub external_id: Option<String>,   // "externalId"
}

pub struct ClaimsPayload {
    pub sub: Option<String>,
    pub account_name: Option<String>,  // "accountName"
    pub sid: Option<String>,
    pub memberships: Vec<Membership>,
}
```

Field for field the same as Go's `StandardClaims`. Every field is optional in the type: parsing says *what the header says*, the service says *whether that is enough*.

---

## A request end to end

`digital-twin`'s extractor, `adapters/driving/http_api/claims.rs`; the others follow the same steps.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant E as Edge
        participant X as digital-twin<br/>GatewayClaims extractor
        participant C as claims-schema
        participant A as digital-twin<br/>service/authz.rs
        E->>X: request + x-gateway-claims
        X->>X: headers.get(CLAIMS_HEADER). none? 401 Missing
        X->>X: to_str(). not text? 401 Invalid
        X->>C: decode_claims_header(raw)
        C->>C: try STANDARD, URL_SAFE, STANDARD_NO_PAD, URL_SAFE_NO_PAD
        C-->>X: bytes, or None then 401 Invalid
        X->>C: serde_json::from_slice into ClaimsPayload
        C->>C: lenient_memberships drops bad entries
        C-->>X: ClaimsPayload, or error then 401 Invalid
        X->>C: payload.user_id()
        C-->>X: trimmed sub, or None then 401 Invalid
        X->>A: GatewayClaims with payload and raw header
        A->>C: payload.memberships, roles
        A->>A: Cedar decision, see Auth Policy

### Data at each step

Header for `{"sub":"u1","accountName":"ada","memberships":[{"domain":"acme","role":"business_admin"},"beta"]}`, sent URL-safe without padding.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `x-gateway-claims: eyJzdWIiOiJ1MSIs…` (no `=` at the end) |
| 5 | `STANDARD` fails on the missing padding; `URL_SAFE` fails too; `STANDARD_NO_PAD` succeeds |
| 6 | The JSON bytes above |
| 8 | `"beta"` is not an object: dropped. `memberships` = `[{acme, business_admin}]` |
| 9 | `ClaimsPayload { sub: Some("u1"), account_name: Some("ada"), sid: None, memberships: [1 entry] }` |
| 11 | `Some("u1")` |
| 12 | `GatewayClaims` with the payload and the raw header, kept so the service can forward it |

---

## Two reading choices

### Four base64 alphabets

`decode_claims_header` tries four decoders in order and keeps the first that works:

| Order | Alphabet | Padding |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `STANDARD` (`+ /`) | required |
| 2 | `URL_SAFE` (`- _`) | required |
| 3 | `STANDARD_NO_PAD` | none |
| 4 | `URL_SAFE_NO_PAD` | none |

A Rust decoder accepts exactly one form. The edge may write any of them, so trying only one would refuse good headers.

### Bad memberships are dropped, not fatal

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M{"memberships field"} -- "missing or null" --> E["empty list"]
        M -- "not a list, like a string" --> F["whole payload fails"]
        M -- list --> L["for each entry"]
        L --> O{"object with a string domain?"}
        O -- no --> D["drop entry"]
        O -- yes --> K["keep: role and externalId optional"]

**Example.** `["acme", {"domain":7}, {"role":"admin"}, {"domain":"beta"}]` keeps only `beta`, with no role.

Why: failing the whole header turns "not a member of that domain" (403) into "broken token" (401). The person debugging then looks in the wrong service. A `memberships` that is not a list at all is still fatal: that is a broken token.

---

## Accessors

| Method | Gives | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `decode(header)` | `decode_claims_header` then JSON parse; `None` on either failure | |
| `user_id()` | `sub`, trimmed; `None` when missing or blank | `{"sub":"   "}` gives `None` |
| `account()` | `accountName`, trimmed, same rule | |
| `session_id()` | `sid`, same rule | `{"sid":""}` gives `None` |
| `domains()` | Every membership's domain | `["acme", "unibo"]` |
| `role_in(domain)` | Role of the first membership with that domain | `role_in("acme")` = `Some("business_admin")` |
| `belongs_to(domain)` | `true` when a membership has that domain; always `false` for `""` | `belongs_to("")` = `false` |
| `has_role(role)` | `true` when any membership has that role, in any domain | |

A blank string is treated as missing, so `"   "` can never act as an identity.

---

## What stays in each service

- **The extractor and its error type.** Axum's `FromRequestParts` is a trait from another crate, and Rust forbids implementing it for this crate's types from a service. The error types also differ: `StatusCode` in `dashboard`, `DomainError` in the others.
- **Access rules.** Cedar entities, the `system:` callers, which field is required. A shape crate that grows rules ties every service to every other.

---

## Tests

| Test | Checks |
|---|---|
| `src/lib.rs` | All four alphabets decode; not-base64 and not-JSON fail; the full contract round trips; blank fields are `None`; missing or `null` memberships give an empty list; bad entries dropped; a non-list fails; roles scoped per domain; a membership with no role still grants the domain |
| `tests/conformance.rs` | `standard-claims.json` parses with `sub`, `accountName`, `sid`, role, `externalId`; written back it equals the fixture, so no field is silently lost; it matches `schemas/json/standard-claims.schema.json`; the schema refuses a role off the ladder (`emperor`) |
| `tests/tenancy_domains_schema.rs` | Fixture-against-schema for `tenancy-domains.json`, a shape between `tenancy` and the frontend with no Rust type |
| `tests/chat_conversation_schema.rs` | Same for `chat-conversation.json`, which only `chat` writes and the frontend reads |

The same claims fixture is checked by Go (`backend/libs/auth-contracts/conformance_test.go`) and Python (`agent/tests/unit/test_claims_conformance.py`). Socket, notification and chat also have an architecture test that the header name is `claims_schema::CLAIMS_HEADER`, never a literal.

```bash
mise exec -- cargo test --manifest-path schemas/claims-schema/Cargo.toml
```

---

## Building a service that uses it

The dependency is a path, `../../schemas/claims-schema`, so each consumer image builds from the repo root (`cd_context: "."` in `.github/services.json`). Each `Dockerfile` copies the crate before `cargo chef prepare` **and** before each `cargo chef cook`; a path missing during `cook` fails the build half way.

---

## Gaps

- **The other languages accept fewer alphabets.** Go's `RequireMeshClaims` takes standard base64 only, and Python's `agent/app/auth.py` uses `base64.b64decode`, also standard. A header these four alphabets accept in Rust can be refused in `tenancy` or `agent`.
- **`role_in` takes the first match.** If one domain appears twice with two roles, Rust and Go's `RoleIn` give the first, while `tenancy`'s own `roleIn` gives the highest.
